title: "pallet-xcm: hold trapped assets in a dedicated trap account"

doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      Trapped assets are now held by a dedicated trap account instead of being dropped without
      a burn and minted back on claim. `pallet_xcm::DropAssets` deposits every trapped asset
      into the account at the new `pallet_xcm::Config::TrapAccountLocation` and
      `pallet_xcm::ClaimAssets` withdraws them from it again (all or nothing), so the sum of
      balances keeps matching total issuance for everything trapped from now on, and claims no
      longer emit a mint and a burn. When the trap account cannot receive an asset, it is burned,
      recorded as a trap of its own (so the assets successfully moved to the trap account stay
      claimable) and reported by the new `AssetsTrapFailed` event; that trap becomes claimable
      once the asset's issuer (or governance) funds the trap account with it.

      Traps recorded before this change only exist as hashes and cannot be automatically migrated
      into the trap account. `pallet_xcm::migration::v2::MigrateToV2<Runtime>` (pallet storage
      version 1 -> 2) moves them to the new `LegacyAssetTraps` map, where nothing is added anymore
      and claims keep minting them back until the map empties. The migration also funds the trap
      account with the native existential deposit (minted, the only issuance change).

      Runtimes must add `type TrapAccountLocation` to their `pallet_xcm::Config`
      (`pallet_xcm::AccountId32TrapLocation` for chains with 32-byte account ids), fund that
      account with the existential deposit at genesis (`pallet_xcm::Pallet::trap_account()`)
      or add the migration for existing chains.

  - audience: Runtime User
    description: |
      Issuers of non-sufficient `pallet_assets` assets: the trap account can only hold
      `frame_system::Config::MaxConsumers` distinct non-sufficient assets through consumer
      references, so to make your asset reliably recoverable from traps, create its account for
      the trap account once with `assets.touch_other(asset_id, trap_account)`. Such an account
      uses a deposit instead of a consumer reference and is kept when its balance drops to zero.
      If an `AssetsTrapFailed` event is emitted for your asset before you could do the above,
      `touch_other` and `mint` the reported amount into the trap account, after which the
      original owner can claim it.

